			<div class="content" id="post-content-2402053">
									

<p>Thanks for taking the time write all that. :)</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The function of the CSS isn't clarified, and to the extent that I can infer, it's just another type of Scranton Reality Anchor. As such, the new type of item is unnecessary - I wouldn't have had a problem with just using an SRA.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It breaks my suspension of disbelief to think that technology that should be familiar to those reading the document has to explained <em>in the document</em>. Aside from that, it's specifically named to give you an idea of what it does without requiring too much explanation (and that works, because you inferred correctly.) As for not using Scranton Reality Anchors, I just don't like the things, so I'm much happier to come up with something myself. That's really the only reason for not using them. I could include them if this specifically gets me downvotes, but I'd rather not.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Also, how is Sarah reacting to the fact that Niles can't get her things any more?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'd say that's outside the scope for this document, but she can still use Niles, just not whenever she wants. I assume she and Niles would have a rough patch in their relationship (including throwing him in a corner and stomping around in a huff.)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>referring to this particular type of anomaly as a &quot;rare variation&quot; of a class of reality manipulation suggests that (a) all reality benders present in similar ways (or in a range of classifiable ways); and (b) the Foundation has studied so many reality benders that it knows that this is rare in that context. Both of those things suggest too large a population of reality benders for my suspension of disbelief.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's fine, it doesn't break mine in the least. However, it's also largely inconsequential, so I'll take out that phrasing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>how is this 9 year old a sufficiently capable engineer to design and build something that can travel at 700kph without death, injury or destruction? Is this anomalous? Is the Foundation interested?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's not anomalous. She's a child prodigy, but I need to make that clearer.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I think you're implying that Sarah's mother is dead, but how has her father (implied to be living: &quot;Dad says…&quot;) reacted to the fact that his child has been kidnapped?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>He doesn't. He never had a daughter, standard protocol took care of that. However, if I need to specify every time that we made sure no one listed their child as missing by administering amnestics to the scip's parents, I'm just going through the motions and padding the article.</p>
<p>Also, her mother isn't dead. Zippy the mutt that died a year ago was though, and it never came when she asked Niles for it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The interview gives a bit of character, but doesn't reveal anything particularly new, meaning that the story doesn't have much of an ending. I would be tempted to suggest that the Foundation had *permanently* substituted the toy, if only for the ironic frisson that might generate.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>True, will edit.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p><em>Collapse'd. ~Zyn</em></p>

