			<div class="content" id="post-content-2402663">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Thanks for taking the time write all that. :)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You're welcome. And sorry, because I know it's a pain when the first comment up isn't wholly positive. I'm not intending to be on the attack, just sharing my reaction.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Some&nbsp;more&nbsp;thoughts</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Make&nbsp;them&nbsp;disappear&nbsp;like&nbsp;an&nbsp;aircraft&nbsp;engine</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>It breaks my suspension of disbelief to think that technology that should be familiar to those reading the document has to explained in the document.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I absolutely agree, and am definitely not asking for an explanation or footnote. I have a complicated relationship with SRAs, but my thought is that adding new types of devices that do much the same thing is adding to the problem, rather than helping it. Unless there's a different reason you don't like them.</p>
<p>The big issue with reality benders in a Foundation context is that because their powers tend to be limitless, it is very difficult to write sensible containment procedures. A limitless nullifier (SRAs and the like) is one solution, but is very convenient for the writer. Keeping the Type Green happy is another, but leads to accusations of hotels and Mary-Sues etc.</p>
<p>I think I would have enjoyed this article more if it was about how we contain a 9 year old magician using only mundane tech, because the answer becomes quite psychological (and therefore character-driven). How nice can the Foundation be to a kid in that situation, even if they want to? Should they take away Niles with the hope that she won't use her power without being able to ask him? Should they find a way to trick her into thinking that Niles doesn't want to get things for her any more? Should they remove her vocal cords to render her unable to ask?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>enabling it to displace and transport any and all materials</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;any and all&quot; is immediately contradicted by the next sentence. Perhaps &quot;a wide range of&quot; or &quot;any and all inorganic&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm just going through the motions and padding the article</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm fine with that in theory, but the article already mentions amnesticizing the Spring Grove cops and counter-information in the Netherlands. I would have thought that the status of Sarah's parents would be more relevant - and as a reader, I got confused about whether they were alive, existed at all etc, because it seemed an important loose end.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Also, her mother isn't dead</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The reason I took that implication is due to the placement of your first footnote. Because it sits after &quot;deceased&quot; and refers to the incident report (which mentions only Sarah's mother), I made the assumption. I wonder whether the footnote is necessary, as even if readers don't pick that up from the incident report, it's also covered in the interview.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I think that the few small changes have improved this. And I am a sucker for that ending - the &quot;nice nice&quot; Foundation still has no qualms about stealing a child's toy when she's asleep, and never giving it back.</p>

							</div>

								
					
