				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/crayne" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1346995); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1346995&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605884193" alt="Crayne" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1346995)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/crayne" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1346995); return false;" >Crayne</a></span> <span class="odate time_1446734840 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">05 Nov 2015 14:47</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2402688">
									

<blockquote>
<p>And sorry, because I know it's a pain when the first comment up isn't wholly positive. I'm not intending to be on the attack, just sharing my reaction.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I didn't take it as such, and don't worry, it's all part of the deal when you coldpost. :)</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>I absolutely agree, and am definitely not asking for an explanation or footnote. I have a complicated relationship with SRAs, but my thought is that adding new types of devices that do much the same thing is adding to the problem, rather than helping it. Unless there's a different reason you don't like them.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>There is, it's completely irrational. There is absolutely no actual basis to defend my choice not to use everybody's favorite handwaving device and come up with my own. I think mine sound cooler though. ;)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The big issue with reality benders in a Foundation context is that because their powers tend to be limitless, it is very difficult to write sensible containment procedures. A limitless nullifier (SRAs and the like) is one solution, but is very convenient for the writer. Keeping the Type Green happy is another, but leads to accusations of hotels and Mary-Sues etc.</p>
<p>I think I would have enjoyed this article more if it was about how we contain a 9 year old magician using only mundane tech, because the answer becomes quite psychological (and therefore character-driven). How nice can the Foundation be to a kid in that situation, even if they want to? Should they take away Niles with the hope that she won't use her power without being able to ask him? Should they find a way to trick her into thinking that Niles doesn't want to get things for her any more? Should they remove her vocal cords to render her unable to ask?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I dislike any other option that including faux-tech that does what I want it to do, to be honest. Sure, we could operate on her so she could never speak again, but then there wouldn't be any opportunity for testing anymore either.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;any and all&quot; is immediately contradicted by the next sentence. Perhaps &quot;a wide range of&quot; or &quot;any and all inorganic&quot;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Will edit.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I'm fine with that in theory, but the article already mentions amnesticizing the Spring Grove cops and counter-information in the Netherlands. I would have thought that the status of Sarah's parents would be more relevant - and as a reader, I got confused about whether they were alive, existed at all etc, because it seemed an important loose end.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I can certainly add something in, but it really isn't consequential to the article. They never had a daughter, and in time we'll probably provide daily amnestics to SCP-2506 to make her forget she has parents too.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The reason I took that implication is due to the placement of your first footnote. Because it sits after &quot;deceased&quot; and refers to the incident report (which mentions only Sarah's mother), I made the assumption. I wonder whether the footnote is necessary, as even if readers don't pick that up from the incident report, it's also covered in the interview.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You have a point there, and I can see how you'd arrive at the conclusion. I'll think about removing the footnote.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>And I am a sucker for that ending - the &quot;nice nice&quot; Foundation still has no qualms about stealing a child's toy when she's asleep, and never giving it back.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's an ending you provided, you know. But yeah, that was gold, thanks for that. A good sour note in the whole &quot;we're decent to anomalies&quot; thing.</p>
</div>
</div>
