				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sirpudding" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(348648); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=348648&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605884193" alt="sirpudding" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=348648)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sirpudding" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(348648); return false;" >sirpudding</a></span> <span class="odate time_1446743846 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">05 Nov 2015 17:17</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2402780">
									

<p>I'm not an engineer (I don't even have a degree), but:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>If we assume the whole thing has a mass of 100&#160;kg then rolling resistance is going to be:<br />
F=(CRF)N<br />
CRF is the coefficent of rolling force; which is like about 0.010 to 0.015 for a car on concrete. Lawn mower tires aren't made for speed though so it's probably on the high end. We'll say 0.020.<br />
N is the normal force or 100kg(9.8&#160;m/s<sup>2</sup>)=980N<br />
F= 196&#160;N</p>
<p>The formula for air drag is F<sub>d</sub>=(1/2)pv<sup>2</sup>C<sub>d</sub>A<br />
p is air density; we can use 1.293&#160;kg/m<sup>3</sup> here.<br />
C<sub>d</sub> is the drag coefficient which is a dimensionless factor based on aerodynamics; we can use this chart: <a href="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/14ilf1l.svg/220px-14ilf1l.svg.png">https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/14ilf1l.svg/220px-14ilf1l.svg.png</a> to estimate it. Riding lawnmowers aren't very streamlined, so it's probably closer to .8 for angled cube than .09 for a streamlined half-body (like a racing car). Looking at this table of cars: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Automobile_drag_coefficient#Typical_drag_coefficients">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Automobile_drag_coefficient#Typical_drag_coefficients</a>, 0.35 seems reasonable, I think.<br />
A is crossectional area. She's small and she probably built compact (plus lawnmower chassis) so 1&#160;m<sup>2</sup> is probably a safe approximation.</p>
<p>So assuming the 1100&#160;N standard model of the engine, the point in which all thrust is going to overcome drag is:<br />
1100N-196N=(1/2)(1.293&#160;kg/m<sup>3</sup>)v<sup>2</sup>(0.35)(1&#160;m<sup>2</sup>)<br />
Combining terms:<br />
904N= 0.226275&#160;kg/m (v<sup>2</sup>)<br />
3995.138658711745&#160;m<sup>2</sup>/s<sup>2</sup>=v<sup>2</sup><br />
Solving for v:<br />
(4861.341288255441&#160;m<sup>2</sup>/s<sup>2</sup>)<sup>(1/2)</sup>=v=63.20710924185463&#160;m/s<br />
Rounding to 1 significant figure we get 60&#160;m/s or <strong>216&#160;km/h</strong>.</p>
<p>There's also some lift; although we can maybe assume she put in airdams and spoilers and whatnot to minimize lift force and <em>I think</em> we can ignore it.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

